= Accuracy and precision — Technical =

== VIM distinctions ==
VIM terminology distinguishes:

'''Measurement accuracy''' — closeness of agreement between a measured value and a true quantity value.

'''Measurement trueness''' — closeness between the average of an infinite number of replicate measured values and a reference quantity value.

'''Measurement precision''' — closeness of agreement among replicate indications or measured quantity values under specified conditions.

'''Measurement bias''' — estimate of systematic measurement error.

Accuracy is not itself treated as a measurable quantity with a numerical value in VIM3.

== Error model ==
A simplified measurement model can write:

<pre>
x_measured = x_true + b + ε
</pre>

where:
* <code>b</code> represents systematic error/bias;
* <code>ε</code> represents random variation.

Precision primarily characterizes the dispersion of ε under defined conditions.

Trueness relates to b.

Real measurement models are usually more complicated because correction terms and references also have uncertainty.

== Estimating precision ==
For repeated measurements <code>x_i</code>, precision can be summarized by sample standard deviation:

<pre>
s = sqrt( Σ(x_i - x̄)^2 / (n-1) )
</pre>

This estimate is conditional on the repeatability/reproducibility conditions.

A low s does not reveal systematic bias.

== Repeatability and reproducibility precision ==
Repeatability precision measures dispersion under tightly controlled same-condition measurements.

Reproducibility precision evaluates dispersion under intentionally changed conditions, potentially including:
* laboratories;
* operators;
* instruments;
* locations;
* times.

Interlaboratory studies can estimate variance components associated with these sources.

== Uncertainty versus error ==
Exact measurement error is generally unknown because the true value is unknown.

Uncertainty quantification instead characterizes plausible values attributable to the measurand after considering available information.

Corrections for known systematic effects may be applied, while residual uncertainty in the correction remains.

